			title: "Marriage — Problem 14",
			topic: "Marriage",
			factPattern: "In 1989, Charice, a Filipina, and Justine, an American, married in the Philippines. They separated in 1990, and Justine obtained a divorce in the United States that same year. He then married Lea in Canada, and they had a son, James. Charice, after failing to hear from Justine, married Bugay, a Filipino, in 1993, and they had a daughter, Regine. Years later, Regine married James in California, where such a marriage is valid.",
			question: "(a) Assuming Justine's divorce and the American law authorizing it are both properly proven, what is its effect on Charice's capacity to remarry? (b) What is the status of the marriage between Regine and James? Discuss, using the ALAC method.",
			modelAnswer: {
				answer: "<p><b>(a) Effect of the divorce on Charice</b></p><p>Charice becomes capacitated to remarry.</p><p><b>(b) Regine and James</b></p><p>The marriage between Regine and James is not barred on account of any blood relationship, since they are not related by consanguinity at all.</p>",
				legalBasis: "<p><b>(a) Effect of the divorce on Charice</b></p><p>The law provides that where a marriage between a Filipino and a foreigner is validly celebrated, and the alien spouse thereafter obtains a divorce abroad capacitating him to remarry, the Filipino spouse is likewise capacitated to remarry.</p><p><b>(b) Regine and James</b></p><p>The law provides that marriages are void for incest only between specified relatives by blood or affinity; two people who share no blood relationship are not covered by these impediments merely because their respective parents were both, at different times, married to a common person.</p>",
				application: "<p><b>(a) Effect of the divorce on Charice</b></p><p>Since it was Justine, the alien spouse, who obtained the divorce, and assuming it and the underlying American law are properly proven, Charice — the Filipino spouse — becomes capacitated to remarry under this rule.</p><p><b>(b) Regine and James</b></p><p>Regine is the daughter of Charice and Bugay, while James is the son of Justine and Lea — Regine and James share no blood relationship whatsoever; their parents' entangled marital history involving Justine does not itself create a prohibited degree of relationship between Regine and James.</p>",
				conclusion: "<p><b>(a) Effect of the divorce on Charice</b></p><p>Charice's subsequent marriage to Bugay may be treated as valid, rather than bigamous, once the divorce and the foreign law behind it are properly established.</p><p><b>(b) Regine and James</b></p><p>The marriage between Regine and James is not void for incest or public-policy reasons on this basis and, assuming all other requisites of marriage are met, may be considered valid.</p>",
			}
